+++
title: Pella
description: Capital of the ancient kingdom of Macedon
hook: Philip II was born at Pella in 382 BC and his son Alexander the Great in
  356 BC. It was a port then, joined to the Thermaic Gulf by a navigable
  inlet; the inlet silted up, and the site now sits inland.
standfirst: Pella lies 1 km outside the modern town of the same name, in
  Central Macedonia, Greece. Digging since 1957 has turned up gridded streets,
  pebble-mosaic floors, a palace of 75,000 m^^2^^ and a lead curse tablet
  still argued over for what it says about the Macedonians' Greek.
key-facts:
  - name: Capital of Macedonia
    value: From 399 BC to 168 BC
  - name: Where it is
    value: Pella, Central Macedonia, Greece
  - name: Its Greek name
    value: Πέλλα
  - name: Language spoken there
    value: Ancient Greek
  - name: Height above sea level
    value: 38 metre
  - name: Museum website
    value: pella-museum.gr
pull-quotes:
  - text: It is situated on the south-west slope of a hill and surrounded by a
      marsh too deep to be crossed on foot either in summer or winter.
    attribution: Livy, reporting how Pella looked in 167 BC to Lucius Aemilius
      Paulus Macedonicus
    section: history
  - text: now insignificant, with very few inhabitants
    attribution: Lucian of Samosata, in about AD 180
    section: history
timeline:
  - when: about 408 BC
    what: Euripides' Bacchae is first staged at Pella.
  - when: 399 BC
    what: Pella becomes capital of Macedonia.
  - when: 382 BC
    what: Philip II is born at Pella.
  - when: c. 375–350 BC
    what: The Pella curse tablet is inscribed on a lead scroll.
  - when: 356 BC
    what: Alexander the Great, Philip's son, is born at Pella.
  - when: 350-330 BC
    what: The oldest parts of the palace are built, in the time of Philip II.
  - when: 325–300 BC
    what: The House of Dionysos is built.
  - when: c. 240 BC
    what: The poet Aratus dies at Pella.
  - when: 168 BC
    what: The Romans sack the city and carry its treasury to Rome; Pella ceases to
      be the capital of Macedonia.
  - when: about 90 BC
    what: An earthquake destroys the city; it is eventually rebuilt over its ruins.
  - when: 58 BC
    what: Cicero stays at Pella, by which time the provincial seat has moved to
      Thessalonica.
  - when: between 45 and 30 BC
    what: Pella is promoted to a Roman colony, its currency marked Colonia Iulia
      Augusta Pella.
  - when: about AD 180
    what: Lucian of Samosata calls it "now insignificant, with very few
      inhabitants".
  - when: 1914–15
    what: G. Oikonomos begins the first excavation.
  - when: "1953"
    what: Modern systematic exploration of the site begins.
  - when: "1957"
    what: Excavations by the Greek Archaeological Service uncover houses with
      mosaic floors.
  - when: "1986"
    what: The Pella curse tablet is found; it is published in the Hellenic
      Dialectology Journal in 1993.
  - when: February 2006
    what: A farmer accidentally uncovers the largest tomb ever found in Greece.
  - when: "2010"
    what: 37 tombs dating from 650 to 280 BC are discovered.
  - when: December, 2025
    what: The restored Palace of Pella opens to the public.
image-plan:
  - file: 0_Mosaïque_de_la_chasse_au_lion_-_Musée_archéologique_de_Pella.JPG
    role: hero
    section: lead
    caption: "The lion hunt mosaic: excavation from 1957 onwards found mosaic
      floors portraying such scenes in the city's large, well-built houses."
  - file: Plan_Pella-en.svg
    role: full
    section: hippodamian-plan
    caption: "Schematic plan of Pella: parallel streets crossing at right angles
      in eight rows of blocks, with the agora taking up ten of them."
  - file: Place_of_the_Dionysos_Mosaic_in_the_House_of_Dionysos,_built_in_325-300_BC,_Ancient_Pella_(7060180819).jpg
    role: inline
    section: history
    caption: The House of Dionysus, built in 325–300 BC, in the decades after
      Cassander redesigned and expanded the city.
  - file: The_main_courtyard_of_the_House_of_Dionysos,_built_in_325-300_BC,_Ancient_Pella_(6914026352).jpg
    role: inline
    section: urban-area
    caption: The main courtyard of the House of Dionysos. Pella's houses were
      enclosed, with frescoed walls round inner courtyards like this one.
  - file: Shops_right_along_the_eastern_edge_of_the_agora,_Ancient_Pella_(7060295407).jpg
    role: inline
    section: urban-area
    caption: Shops along the eastern edge of the agora, the seven-hectare centre
      of the city built in the last quarter of the 4th century BC.
  - file: Stag_Hunt_Mosaic_from_the_House_of_the_Abduction_of_Helen,_(c._300_BC),_Ancient_Pella_(6913868698).jpg
    role: full
    section: urban-area
    caption: The Stag Hunt Mosaic, c. 300 BC, from the House of the Abduction of
      Helen — one of the rich houses named after the mosaics on its floors.
  - file: Pella_leaded_tablet_(katadesmos)_4th_Century_retouched.jpg
    role: full
    section: ancient-macedonian-in-pella-curse-tablet
    caption: "The Pella curse tablet in the Archaeological Museum of Pella: a
      curse inscribed on a lead scroll, dated to c. 375–350 BC."
  - file: Aerial_photograph_and_plan_of_the_House_of_Dionysos,_Archaeological_Museum,_Pella_(7065301481).jpg
    role: inline
    section: palace-of-pella
    caption: Plan and aerial photograph of the House of Dionysos, showing the
      rooms ranged around a central courtyard.
  - file: Pella_baths.jpg
    role: inline
    section: palace-of-pella
    caption: Bathtubs in the public baths. Archaeologists date the baths and the
      palaestra to the reign of Cassander.
glossary:
  - term: agora
    gloss: The central public space of a Greek city; Pella's covered about 7
      hectares, or ten city blocks, and was reached by the wide East–West
      street.
  - term: stoa
    gloss: A shaded colonnade; stoas ran round the edges of Pella's agora.
  - term: Hippodamian plan
    gloss: "The grid plan envisaged by Hippodamus: parallel streets intersecting
      at right angles to form rows of rectangular blocks."
  - term: katadesmos
    gloss: Greek for a curse — at Pella, one inscribed on a lead scroll and buried.
  - term: Phacus
    gloss: The citadel close to Pella, standing in the marsh itself like an island
      and joined to the city by a single bridge.
  - term: propylaeum
    gloss: A monumental gateway; the palace had a triple one, 15 m high, breaking
      the line of its south portico.
  - term: trompe-l'œil
    gloss: Wall painting that imitates a perspective view; the first such murals
      ever seen were at Pella.
  - term: duumvirs quinquennales
    gloss: The colonial magistrates of the Roman city; four pairs of them are known.
further:
  - slug: macedonia-ancient-kingdom
    why: Pella was its capital from the 4th century BC until the Roman conquest of
      168 BC.
  - slug: alexander-the-great
    why: He was born here in 356 BC, in the city his father Philip II had walled
      and made internationally important.
